course: dictionary
lesson: 4913
title: Immature: not yet grown up
level: primary-5
stars: 2
explain: Immature has two meanings, and the context tells you which one you need. In everyday talk it means silly and childish, not as sensible as you should be for your age. «Stop being so immature!» is what you might hear when someone acts like a much younger child.
explain: In science it means not fully grown or developed yet. A young animal that is not ready to live on its own, or a fruit that is not yet ripe, can be called immature. Ask yourself: is the sentence about behaviour, or about growing?
explain: Look at the words around immature for clues. Words like behaviour or emotionally point to the first meaning. Words about animals, plants or cells point to the second. Then swap in a simpler word, such as childish or not yet ripe, and check that the sentence still makes sense.
fact: Immature means behaving in a silly, childish way, or not yet fully grown or developed.
fact: «Immature behaviour» means acting younger than your age.
fact: «Emotionally immature» means not yet good at handling feelings in a grown-up way.
fact: The opposite of immature is mature.
fact: The prefix im- means not. It is used before words starting with m or p, such as immature, impossible and impatient.
exercise: «Stop being so immature!» What does immature mean here? | Very clever for your age | Fully grown and ripe | *Silly and childish, not as sensible as you should be | Quiet and shy
exercise: The fruit was still immature, so it was hard and green. What does immature mean here? | Very old | Cut into slices | *Not yet fully grown or ripe | Sweet and juicy
exercise: Which word is the opposite of immature? | Imaginary | Impossible | *Mature | Impatient
test: «Immature behaviour» means behaviour that is | Sleepy and slow | Very careful and grown-up | Shiny and bright | *Childish and not as sensible as it should be
test: The prefix im- in immature means | *Not | Very | Under | Again
exam: Which meaning fits «The immature plum was hard and sour.»? | Silly and childish | Wise and old | *Not yet fully ripe | Very large
